// std.core.scope
//
// `@scope` is the enclosing block as a value — what a LoopHandle is
// to a loop, one level down. It nests, and each block gets its own:
//
//   {
//       outer = @scope;
//       {
//           inner = @scope;
//           inner.defer(() { println("inner cleanup") });
//       }                                    // inner's defers run
//       outer.defer(() { println("outer cleanup") });
//   }                                        // outer's defers run
//
// This is where `defer` lives, and it is why defer needs neither a
// keyword nor ambient authority: the block owns its own stack of
// closures. It is also why Env does not own one — Env outlives
// every block inside main, so a defer stack there would be storage
// in the wrong place.
//
// A Scope is NON-ESCAPING. It may be passed INWARD, so a helper can
// register cleanup on its caller's block, but it can never be
// stored in a struct, returned, or captured by an escaping closure.
// The same escaping/non-escaping distinction that governs `.try()`
// and `h.break()`, doing a third job. Stage 3 is where the checker
// enforces it.
Scope* = {
    // required (`= sig`, no body): the compiler supplies the block.
    //
    // Registers a closure on THIS block. They run LIFO at block
    // exit, BEFORE any drop — a stack, not a map, because order is
    // the point, and it lives with the block that owns it.
    //
    // `:` and not `::`: the closure stack lives in the block, not
    // in the handle's own bytes. A bitwise copy of the handle would
    // see the registration, which is exactly the test.
    defer* = (self: @Self, f: () ()) ()
}
